.SH NAME
ktm-guide-varargs \- Variable arguments (chapter 19 of the ktm language guide)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
A function can take any number of extra arguments after its fixed ones. Inside the function they are a slice, with no \fBva_list\fR to walk. There are three kinds: typed extras (\fBT ...name\fR), extras of any type (\fBany ...name\fR) and untyped 64-bit slots for format functions (\fB\&...name\fR). This chapter covers each, passing extras on to another function, and C's own \fB\&...\fR\&.
.SH Typed extras
.PP
\fBT ...name\fR as the last parameter takes any number of \fBT\fRs, zero included. Each is checked and converted like a normal argument. Inside, \fBname\fR is a \fBT[]\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i64 sum(i64 ...nums) {
    i64 total = 0;
    for (i64 i = 0; i < nums.len; i++) {
        total += nums[i];
    }
    return total;
}

i32 main() {
    printf("%d %d %d\en", sum(), sum(5), sum(1, 2, 3, 4));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
0 5 10
.EE
.RE
.PP
The extras are converted to \fBT\fR, so integers given for \fBf64 ...\fR become floats:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
f64 avg(f64 ...xs) {
    if (!xs.len) {
        return 0;
    }
    f64 total = 0;
    for (i64 i = 0; i < xs.len; i++) {
        total += xs[i];
    }
    return total / xs.len;
}

i32 main() {
    printf("%.2f %.2f %.2f\en", avg(1, 2, 4), avg(2.5), avg());
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
2.33 2.50 0.00
.EE
.RE
.PP
An argument of the wrong type is refused like for any parameter:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
i64 sum(i64 ...nums) {
    return nums.len;
}

i32 main() {
    return sum(1, "two");
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
argument 2 of `sum`: expected i64, got u8[4]
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBT\fR is a number type or a pointer type. Structs travel as pointers:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Point { i64 x, y; };

Point centroid(Point * ...ps) {
    Point c = {0, 0};
    for (i64 i = 0; i < ps.len; i++) {
        c.x += ps[i].x;
        c.y += ps[i].y;
    }
    c.x /= ps.len;
    c.y /= ps.len;
    return c;
}

i32 main() {
    Point a = {0, 0}, b = {4, 0}, c = {2, 6};
    Point m = centroid(&a, &b, &c);
    printf("(%d, %d)\en", m.x, m.y);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
(2, 2)
.EE
.RE
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
struct Point { i64 x, y; };

void take(Point ...ps) {
}

i32 main() {
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
must be numbers or pointers (or `any ...ps`), got Point
.EE
.RE
.PP
For values of mixed or struct types, use \fBany ...name\fR (below).
.SS Using the slice
.IP \(bu 2
\fBname.len\fR is how many extras were passed; \fBname[i]\fR is one of them, bounds-checked.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBname\fR can be sliced, looped over and passed to a \fBT[]\fR parameter.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBname\fR itself can't be assigned.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i64 largest(i64[] xs) {
    i64 best = xs[0];
    for (i64 i = 1; i < xs.len; i++) {
        if (xs[i] > best) {
            best = xs[i];
        }
    }
    return best;
}

i64 maxOf(i64 first, i64 ...rest) {
    if (!rest.len) {
        return first;
    }
    i64 m = largest(rest);
    return m > first ? m : first;
}

i32 main() {
    printf("%d %d\en", maxOf(3), maxOf(3, 9, 4));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
3 9
.EE
.RE
.SH Fixed and default parameters first
.PP
The extras come after every fixed parameter, defaults included. To give extras while keeping a default, leave that argument empty:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void tag(u8 *label, u8 *sep = ", ", u8 * ...items) {
    printf("%s:", label);
    for (i64 i = 0; i < items.len; i++) {
        printf("%s%s", i ? sep : " ", items[i]);
    }
    printf("\en");
}

i32 main() {
    tag("fruit", , "apple", "pear");
    tag("path", "/", "usr", "lib", "ktm");
    tag("empty");
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
fruit: apple, pear
path: usr/lib/ktm
empty:
.EE
.RE
.SH Passing them on
.PP
\fB\&...name\fR as the last argument of a call passes a function's extras on to another function that takes the same kind. It must be the only extra argument of that call:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i64 sum(i64 ...nums) {
    i64 t = 0;
    for (i64 i = 0; i < nums.len; i++) {
        t += nums[i];
    }
    return t;
}

i64 sumTwice(i64 ...nums) {
    return 2 * sum(...nums);
}

i32 main() {
    printf("%d\en", sumTwice(1, 2, 3));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
12
.EE
.RE
.PP
A slice can be spread the same way, so part of the extras can be passed on:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
f64 avg(f64 ...xs) {
    f64 total = 0;
    for (i64 i = 0; i < xs.len; i++) {
        total += xs[i];
    }
    return xs.len ? total / xs.len : 0;
}

f64 avgWithoutFirst(f64 ...xs) {
    return avg(...xs[1:]);
}

i32 main() {
    printf("%.2f\en", avgWithoutFirst(100, 1, 2));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1.50
.EE
.RE
.PP
Spreading costs nothing: the extras travel as a count and a pointer to the array the caller built.
.SH Extras of any type
.PP
\fBany ...name\fR takes extras of any type, like Go's \fB\&...any\fR\&. Inside, \fBname\fR is a \fBreflect.Value[]\fR: each extra's type, and the address of a copy made at the call, which lives until the calling function returns. A string literal or an array comes as a slice. It needs \fBimport reflect;\fR (see Reflection (see ktm-guide-reflection(7))).
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import reflect;

struct Point { i64 x, y; };

void show(any ...args) {
    for (i64 i = 0; i < args.len; i++) {
        reflect.Value v = args[i];
        printf("%.*s", v.type.name.len, v.type.name.ptr);
        if (v.type.kind == reflect.Kind.int) {
            printf(" %d", *(i64 *)v.ptr);
        }
        printf("\en");
    }
}

i32 main() {
    Point p = {1, 2};
    show(42, "text", 2.5, p, &p);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
i64 42
u8[]
f64
Point
Point *
.EE
.RE
.PP
A function that formats whatever it gets can switch on the kind:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import reflect;

void describe(any ...args) {
    for (i64 i = 0; i < args.len; i++) {
        reflect.Value v = args[i];
        if (v.type.kind == reflect.Kind.int) {
            printf("int %d", *(i64 *)v.ptr);
        } else if (v.type.kind == reflect.Kind.float) {
            printf("float %.1f", *(f64 *)v.ptr);
        } else if (v.type.kind == reflect.Kind.slice) {
            u8[] s = *(u8[] *)v.ptr;
            printf("text %.*s", s.len, s.ptr);
        } else {
            printf("a %.*s", v.type.name.len, v.type.name.ptr);
        }
        printf(i + 1 < args.len ? ", " : "\en");
    }
}

i32 main() {
    describe(1, 2.5, "three", true);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
int 1, float 2.5, text three, a bool
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\&...name\fR passes them on to another \fBany\fR function.
.IP \(bu 2
A \fBString\fR returned by a call is refused as an \fBany\fR extra, since nothing would free it: put it in a variable first.
.IP \(bu 2
The library takes \fBany ...\fR where Go takes \fB\&...any\fR: SQL parameters, log fields.
.SH Untyped extras, for formats
.PP
\fB\&...name\fR without a type takes each extra as a 64-bit slot, the way format functions want them. \fBname\fR is a \fBu64[]\fR: a number as it is, a float as its bits, a pointer as its address. \fBcstr.formatArgs\fR formats them like \fBprintf\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import cstr, mem;

void logf(u8 *fmt, ...args) {
    u8 *msg = cstr.formatArgs(null, fmt, args);
    printf("[log] %s\en", msg);
    mem.free(msg);
}

i32 main() {
    logf("%d files in %s, %.1f%% full", 3, "/tmp", 42.5);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
[log] 3 files in /tmp, 42.5% full
.EE
.RE
.PP
Nothing checks these extras against the format: the function's own code decides what each slot is, so a wrong count or type shows only at run time.
.PP
A \fBString\fR or a \fBu8[]\fR goes to such a function as it goes to \fBprintf\fR, when its format parameter is called \fBfmt\fR (right before \fB\&...args\fR): with a literal format, \fB%s\fR prints its bytes (a \fBu8[]\fR needs no terminating zero).
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import cstr, mem, str;

void reply(u8 *fmt, ...args) {
    u8 *msg = cstr.formatArgs(null, fmt, args);
    printf("> %s\en", msg);
    mem.free(msg);
}

i32 main() {
    scoped String user = str.from("ada");
    u8[] cmd = "GET key";
    reply("%s ran %s", user, cmd[0:3]);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
> ada ran GET
.EE
.RE
.PP
To read a slot by hand, an integer is the slot itself and a float is read back through a pointer:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void show(...args) {
    printf("%d slots: %d and %.2f\en", args.len, args[0], *(f64 *)&args[1]);
}

i32 main() {
    show(7, 2.5);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
2 slots: 7 and 2.50
.EE
.RE
.SH C's ...
.PP
A plain \fB\&...\fR with no name is C's variadic convention, for declaring C functions that take one (see Calling C (see ktm-guide-calling-c(7))):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
extern "c" i32 snprintf(u8 *buf, u64 size, u8 *fmt, ...);

i32 main() {
    u8 buf[32];
    snprintf(buf, 32, "%d\-%s", 7, "seven");
    printf("%s\en", buf);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
7\-seven
.EE
.RE
.SH Which kind to use
.PP
.TS
allbox tab(	);
lb lb lb
l l lx .
