T}	T{
Use it for
T}
T{
\fBT ...name\fR
T}	T{
a \fBT[]\fR
T}	T{
many numbers or pointers of one type: numbers to add, paths to join
T}
T{
\fBany ...name\fR
T}	T{
a \fBreflect.Value[]\fR
T}	T{
values of any type, looked at one by one
T}
T{
\fB\&...name\fR
T}	T{
a \fBu64[]\fR of slots
T}	T{
formats passed on to \fBcstr.formatArgs\fR
T}
T{
\fB\&...\fR (no name)
T}	T{
nothing
T}	T{
declaring a C function
T}
.TE
.SH Rules
.IP \(bu 2
The extras parameter is the last one; fixed and default parameters come first, and an empty argument keeps a default.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBT ...xs\fR: \fBT\fR is a number or pointer type; each extra is checked and converted to \fBT\fR; \fBxs\fR is a \fBT[]\fR that can't be assigned.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBany ...xs\fR: \fBxs\fR is a \fBreflect.Value[]\fR of copies made at the call; it needs \fBimport reflect;\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\&...xs\fR: \fBxs\fR is a \fBu64[]\fR of 64-bit slots, not checked against any format.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBf(...xs)\fR passes extras (or a slice) on; it is the only extra argument of that call, and can't go to an overloaded function.
